("RK45","Runge–Kutta 4(5)","Embedded Runge–Kutta pair that compares fourth- and fifth-order solutions to adapt the step size.",28),
("CFAR","constant false-alarm rate","Detection threshold scaled by a local noise estimate so the false-alarm probability stays fixed as the background changes.",26),
("MDL","minimum description length","Model-order selection criterion with a heavier penalty than AIC; more conservative for long records.",27),
("NW","time–half-bandwidth product","Multitaper design parameter: N samples times half-bandwidth W; about 2NW − 1 DPSS tapers are well concentrated.",27,[27]),
("QL","QL algorithm","Eigenvalue iteration like QR but with a lower-triangular factor; the standard method for symmetric tridiagonal matrices such as the DPSS problem.",27,[27]),
# --- beamforming, imaging, arrays ---
("AF","array factor","The far-field beam pattern of the array weights alone (isotropic elements); for a uniform linear array it is the DTFT of the apodization.",4),
("GL","grating lobe","A replica of the main lobe produced by spatial undersampling, when the pitch exceeds λ/(1 + |sin θ₀|) for steering angle θ₀.",4),
("EF","element factor","The directivity of a single element; the array beam pattern is the array factor times the element factor.",19,[19]),
("BF","beamforming (beamformer)","Combining channel signals with delays and weights to focus on each image point.",23),
("SL","sidelobe (level)","Energy outside the main lobe of a beam pattern; its level in dB relative to the peak.",23,[23]),
("EMI","electromagnetic interference","Unwanted electrical pickup from external sources; coherent across firings, so averaging does not remove it.",23),
("ERM","exploding reflector model","Approximation (Garcia et al. 2013) that treats plane-wave data as if each scatterer emitted at time zero in a medium with an adjusted speed, so seismic f–k migration applies.",24),
("ZO","zero-offset","Seismic term for coincident source and receiver (monostatic data), so delays are 2r/c.",24,[24]),
("INR","interference-to-noise ratio","Power of an interfering signal relative to the noise; adaptive nulls deepen as it rises.",25,[25]),
("ICA","independent component analysis","Blind decomposition of data into statistically independent components; used to compress ADMIRE's model matrices for speed.",25,[25]),
("iADMIRE","iterative ADMIRE","ADMIRE variant (Schlunk et al. 2021) that iterates the model fit so weak tissue next to bright targets is not shrunk away.",25),
("CMPE","common-midpoint phase error","Sound-speed autofocus loss that compares transmit–receive pairs sharing a common midpoint, whose speckle should match when the speed map is right.",32),
("QUASOS","Quantitative Ultrasound estimation of Attenuation and Speed-Of-Sound","IEEE IUS 2026 challenge that scored attenuation and sound-speed estimators on shared phantom and simulated data for bias, variance, detectability and robustness to aberration.",32),
# --- transducers, materials, fabrication ---
("IL","insertion loss","Loss in dB between the electrical power available to a transducer and the power delivered (one-way) or received back (two-way, pulse-echo).",16),
("ML1","matching layer 1","The matching layer next to the piezoelectric plate (highest impedance of the stack).",16),
("ML2","matching layer 2","The second matching layer, between ML1 and the tissue or lens.",16),
("FE","finite element","Mesh-based numerical modelling (FEM/FEA); it captures lateral modes, kerf crosstalk and lens effects that 1D models miss.",17),
("PZFlex","PZFlex","Commercial explicit time-domain finite-element code for piezoelectric transducers and wave propagation, now part of the OnScale/Ansys family.",21),
("OnScale","OnScale","Cloud-based successor of PZFlex for explicit finite-element transducer simulation, now part of Ansys.",21),
("COMSOL","COMSOL Multiphysics","Commercial multiphysics finite-element package used for coupled piezoelectric, structural and acoustic simulations.",17),
("FOCUS","Fast Object-oriented C++ Ultrasound Simulator","Field simulator (McGough, Michigan State) using the fast nearfield method and time–space decomposition for fast, accurate linear pressure fields.",21),
("TSD","time–space decomposition","FOCUS technique that separates the excitation's time dependence from the spatial integrals to compute transient fields quickly.",21),
("FD2","second-order finite difference","Finite-difference stencil whose error falls as the square of the grid step; needs more points per wavelength than FD4.",21),
("FD4","fourth-order finite difference","Wider finite-difference stencil whose error falls as the fourth power of the grid step, giving less numerical dispersion per grid point.",21),
("AC","alternating current","Time-varying signal component; in a CMUT a small AC drive rides on the DC bias, and AC poling uses an alternating field to pole crystals.",18),
("FOM","figure of merit","A single number combining material or device properties to rank options, e.g. transmit or receive merit of transducer materials.",18),
("KNN","potassium sodium niobate","(K,Na)NbO₃, a lead-free piezoelectric ceramic studied as a PZT replacement.",18,[18]),
("BNT","bismuth sodium titanate","(Bi₀.₅Na₀.₅)TiO₃, a lead-free piezoelectric ceramic family.",18),
("BKT","bismuth potassium titanate","(Bi₀.₅K₀.₅)TiO₃, a lead-free piezoelectric ceramic family.",18),
("PMN","lead magnesium niobate","Pb(Mg₁/₃Nb₂/₃)O₃; in this course mostly shorthand for the PMN-PT single crystal.",16,[16,18]),
("PIN","lead indium niobate","Pb(In₁/₂Nb₁/₂)O₃; added in ternary PIN-PMN-PT crystals to raise the Curie temperature and coercive field.",16,[16,18]),
("PT","lead titanate","PbTiO₃; its fraction (about 28–33 % in PMN-PT) places the crystal near the morphotropic phase boundary, where coupling peaks.",16,[16,18]),
("SOI","silicon on insulator","Wafer with a thin silicon device layer on a buried oxide over a handle wafer; in wafer-bonded CMUTs the device layer becomes the membrane.",18),
("BOX","buried oxide","The oxide layer of an SOI wafer between the device layer and the handle.",18,[18]),
("BEOL","back end of line","The metal interconnect layers of a CMOS chip, built after the transistors; CMUTs can be fabricated on top of them.",18),
("DRIE","deep reactive ion etching","Plasma etch that cuts deep, near-vertical features in silicon, e.g. PMUT cavities and through-wafer vias.",18),
("LOCOS","local oxidation of silicon","Growing thick oxide only in selected areas; used in some CMUT processes to define the gap and posts.",18),
("SEM","scanning electron microscope","Microscope that images surfaces with a focused electron beam at nanometre resolution.",18),
("PCB","printed circuit board","Board with copper traces that carries and connects electronic components.",18),
("ICE","intracardiac echocardiography","Cardiac imaging from a catheter placed inside the heart chambers.",18),
# --- front end, electronics ---
("dBFS","decibels relative to full scale","Level in dB relative to the largest signal an ADC can represent without clipping; ADC SNR is usually quoted this way.",20),
("dBV","decibels relative to 1 volt","Voltage level 20·log₁₀(V / 1 V); −96 dBV is about 16 µV.",20),
("dBc","decibels relative to the carrier","Level of a harmonic or spurious tone in dB relative to the fundamental.",20),
("MS","megasamples","MS/s means 10⁶ samples per second, the unit of ADC and oscilloscope sampling rates.",20,[20,21]),
("AFE","analog front end","Integrated circuit combining LNA, VGA, anti-aliasing filter and ADC (often with a CW mixer) for 8–16 receive channels.",20),
("LSB","least significant bit","The smallest step an ADC resolves: the full-scale range divided by 2 to the number of bits.",20),
("SINAD","signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio","Power of a test tone over all noise and distortion power; ENOB = (SINAD − 1.76 dB)/6.02 dB.",20),
("IC","integrated circuit","A chip carrying many transistors and passive parts, such as an analog front end.",20,[20]),
("LVDS","low-voltage differential signalling","Serial digital interface standard used to stream ADC samples to the FPGA.",20),
("JESD204B","JEDEC JESD204B","Serial interface standard for high-speed ADC and DAC data links, carrying several channels per multi-gigabit lane.",20),
("PCIe","PCI Express","Serial point-to-point bus linking acquisition hardware to host memory and GPUs; bandwidth scales with generation and lane count (×8, ×16).",20),
("PWM","pulse-width modulation","Shaping a waveform's average by varying the width of fixed-level pulses; lets switching pulsers approximate apodized or tapered transmits.",20),
("RTZ","return to zero","Pulser drive that returns to 0 V between positive and negative half-cycles (three-level drive).",20),
("SHE","selective harmonic elimination","Choosing the switching instants of a multi-level pulse so that chosen harmonics cancel.",20,[20]),
("EEPROM","electrically erasable programmable read-only memory","Non-volatile memory; in a probe it stores identification and calibration data.",20),
("SARUS","Synthetic Aperture Real-time Ultrasound System","DTU research scanner with 1024 channels that stores all channel data for offline synthetic-aperture processing.",20),
("UARP","Ultrasound Array Research Platform","University of Leeds open research scanner platform with FPGA-based transmit and receive.",20),
# --- acoustic output and safety ---
("SPPA","spatial-peak pulse-average","Short for spatial-peak pulse-average intensity (ISPPA): the highest intensity averaged over the pulse duration; ISPPA.3 is the derated value.",22),
("SP","spatial peak","Subscript for the maximum over the field, as in ISPTA.",22,[22]),
("TA","temporal average","Subscript for an average over the pulse repetition period, as in ISPTA.",22,[22]),
("PA","pulse average","Subscript for an average over the pulse duration, as in ISPPA or IPA.3.",22,[22,36]),
("DF","duty factor","Fraction of time the transducer is transmitting: pulse duration times PRF.",22,[22]),
("OB","obstetric","Obstetric (pregnancy) imaging; output guidance is stricter for OB scans, especially after 10 weeks when fetal bone forms.",22),
("LMP","last menstrual period","Reference date for gestational age; thermal-index guidance uses weeks after the LMP.",22),
("ODS","Output Display Standard","AIUM/NEMA standard (NEMA UD 3) for real-time on-screen display of MI and TI; the basis of FDA Track 3.",22),
("UD","NEMA UD standards","NEMA ultrasound standards: UD 2 for acoustic output measurement and UD 3, the Output Display Standard for MI and TI.",22),
("BMUS","British Medical Ultrasound Society","UK professional society whose safety guidelines give MI and TI thresholds and scanning-time advice.",22),
("ITRUSST","International Transcranial Ultrasonic Stimulation Safety and Standards consortium","Expert consortium that publishes consensus safety recommendations for transcranial ultrasound, including use of the cranial thermal index.",22),
# --- contrast, Doppler, elastography, ULM ---
("PE","peak enhancement","Maximum of the time–intensity curve above baseline; a CEUS perfusion parameter.",28,[28]),
("WiR","wash-in rate","Maximum slope of the rising part of the time–intensity curve.",28),
("AUC","area under the curve","In contrast imaging, the area under the time–intensity curve (a perfusion parameter); in detection studies, the area under the ROC curve.",28),
("AMPI","amplitude modulation plus pulse inversion","Contrast sequence combining amplitude and polarity changes (e.g. +1 and −½) whose sum cancels linear echoes but keeps even-order echoes and odd-order echoes in the fundamental band.",28),
("DPPG","dipalmitoylphosphatidylglycerol","A phospholipid used, with DSPC, in microbubble shells.",28),
("PSV","peak systolic velocity","Highest velocity of the maximum-frequency Doppler envelope during systole.",29),
("EDV","end-diastolic velocity","Velocity of the maximum-frequency Doppler envelope at the end of diastole.",29),
("TO","transverse oscillation","Vector-flow method that shapes the receive apodization to create a lateral oscillation in the PSF, so lateral velocity can be estimated.",29,[29]),
("BPC","blood power spectral Capon","Spectral Doppler estimator using a Capon (minimum-variance) filter bank to sharpen spectra from short ensembles.",29),
("BAPES","blood amplitude and phase estimation","Adaptive spectral Doppler estimator using a bank of matched filters to resolve spectra from few samples.",29),
("CBR","clutter-to-blood ratio","Clutter power relative to blood-signal power, in dB; it sets the stopband attenuation the wall filter needs.",29),
("fUS","functional ultrasound","Imaging of brain activity through blood-volume changes measured by ultrafast power Doppler.",29),
("SV","sample volume (or vertically polarized shear wave)","In Doppler (Module 29): the range-gated region the spectrum comes from. In anisotropic elastography (Module 30): the vertically polarized shear wave, coupled to the tensile modulus.",29,[29,30]),
("SH","horizontally polarized shear wave","In transversely isotropic tissue such as muscle, the shear wave polarized in the fibre plane; its speed depends on angle to the fibres.",30),
("KV","Kelvin–Voigt model","Viscoelastic model of a spring and dashpot in parallel; it predicts shear-wave speed growing without bound with frequency.",30),
("XC","cross-correlation","Cross-correlation time-delay estimate, e.g. of shear-wave arrival.",30,[30]),
("pSWE","point shear wave elastography","Shear-wave method that reports one speed from a small ROI after a push, rather than a 2D map.",30),
("SRU","Society of Radiologists in Ultrasound","US society whose consensus statements, e.g. on liver elastography and carotid stenosis, set clinical criteria.",30),
("cACLD","compensated advanced chronic liver disease","Advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis without decompensation; in the SRU consensus, a shear-wave liver stiffness above about 13 kPa suggests it.",30),
("CSPH","clinically significant portal hypertension","Hepatic venous pressure gradient of 10 mmHg or more; in the SRU consensus, a shear-wave liver stiffness above about 17 kPa makes it probable.",30),
("ECG","electrocardiogram","Recording of the heart's electrical activity, used to gate acquisitions to the cardiac cycle.",30),
("IQR","interquartile range","Spread between the 25th and 75th percentiles; IQR/median ≤ 30 % is the usual reliability check for liver stiffness.",30),
("SNRe","elastographic signal-to-noise ratio","Mean over standard deviation of the strain estimate in a uniform region.",30),
("MRI","magnetic resonance imaging","Tomographic imaging of nuclear magnetic resonance signals; MR elastography uses it to image shear waves.",30),
("MR","magnetic resonance","As in MR elastography, which images shear waves with MRI.",30,[30]),
("CT","computed tomography","X-ray tomographic imaging; in aberration correction, CT gives prior sound-speed and density maps of bone.",33),
("fULM","functional ULM","ULM used to measure activity-driven blood-flow changes at microvascular scale.",31),
("WA","weighted average","Centroid localization: the intensity-weighted mean position inside a small box.",31,[31]),
("RS","radial symmetry","Localization that finds the point closest to all lines along the local intensity gradients.",31,[31]),
("GAU","Gaussian fit","Localization by a three-point Gaussian (log-parabola) fit to the peak.",31,[31]),
("INT","interpolation","Localization by finding the peak of a cubically interpolated (upsampled) image.",31,[31]),
("SUSHI","sparsity-based ultrasound super-resolution hemodynamic imaging","Super-resolution method that recovers microbubble positions by sparse recovery rather than isolating single bubbles.",31),
# --- display chain ---
("DICOM","Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine","The standard (NEMA PS3) for medical image files, networking and display, including ultrasound image objects, region calibration and the grayscale display function.",34),
("PS3","DICOM standard part designation","The DICOM standard as published by NEMA; PS3.n is Part n, e.g. PS3.3 (information objects) and PS3.14 (Grayscale Standard Display Function).",34),
("IOD","information object definition","DICOM specification of the attributes an object, such as an ultrasound image, must or may contain.",34),
("MONOCHROME2","MONOCHROME2","DICOM photometric interpretation for grayscale images in which the minimum value is displayed as black.",34),
("RGB","red, green, blue","Colour encoding with one value per primary; colour ultrasound stored as RGB takes three times the data of MONOCHROME2.",34),
("sRGB","standard RGB","Standard colour space (IEC 61966-2-1) with defined primaries, white point and transfer curve; the default for consumer displays.",34),
("YBR","luminance–chrominance (Y, Cb, Cr)","DICOM colour encoding with one luminance and two colour-difference channels; used with JPEG, where chroma can be subsampled.",34),
("CIE","International Commission on Illumination","Body that defines colorimetry, including the XYZ colour space and the CIELAB lightness L*.",34),
("CIELAB","CIE 1976 L*a*b* colour space","Approximately perceptually uniform colour space; L* is lightness, used to judge whether a colour map changes evenly.",34),
("CVD","colour vision deficiency","Reduced ability to distinguish some colours (roughly 8 % of men, mostly red–green); colour maps should stay readable for it.",34),
("LCD","liquid-crystal display","Display that modulates a backlight with liquid-crystal cells; luminance and contrast change with viewing angle.",34),
("OLED","organic light-emitting diode","Emissive display technology whose pixels emit light and can switch fully off, giving a very low black level.",34),
("JPEG","Joint Photographic Experts Group","Image compression standard; lossy JPEG quantizes 8 × 8 block DCT coefficients and can remove fine speckle and edge detail.",34),
("DCT","discrete cosine transform","Real-valued Fourier-related transform; the block transform inside JPEG compression.",34),
("QA","quality assurance","Planned, periodic tests (display, probe or array checks) that verify a system still meets its specifications.",34),
("AAPM","American Association of Physicists in Medicine","Society whose task-group reports (e.g. TG18, Report 270) define display quality-control tests.",34),
("TG18","AAPM Task Group 18","AAPM report (2005) and test patterns for assessing medical display performance: luminance response, uniformity, resolution and noise.",34),
("ACR","American College of Radiology","Professional body that, with AAPM and SIIM, publishes the technical standard for electronic display of medical images.",34),
("SIIM","Society for Imaging Informatics in Medicine","Professional society for imaging informatics; co-author of the ACR–AAPM–SIIM display standard.",34),
("NEMA","National Electrical Manufacturers Association","US association that publishes DICOM (as NEMA PS3) and the ultrasound UD standards.",34),
("US","ultrasound (DICOM modality)","DICOM modality code for ultrasound, as in the US Image and US Region Calibration modules.",34,[34]),
# --- image enhancement ---
("PSNR","peak signal-to-noise ratio","10·log₁₀ of the squared peak value over the mean squared error to a reference image; misleading for speckle, which has no clean reference.",35),
("SSIM","structural similarity index","Similarity measure combining local luminance, contrast and structure against a reference (Wang et al. 2004); like PSNR, it misleads for speckle.",35),
("HE","histogram equalization","Mapping intensities through their cumulative distribution so the output histogram is approximately flat.",35,[35]),
("CLAHE","contrast-limited adaptive histogram equalization","Histogram equalization in tiles with a clip limit on each histogram, blended between tiles, to raise local contrast without excessive noise amplification.",35),
("CDF","cumulative distribution function","Probability that a random variable is at or below a given value.",35),
("SRAD","speckle-reducing anisotropic diffusion","Diffusion filter (Yu and Acton 2002) whose diffusion coefficient follows a local speckle statistic, smoothing homogeneous speckle while stopping at edges.",35),
("NLM","non-local means","Denoiser that replaces each pixel by a weighted average of pixels whose surrounding patches look similar, wherever they are in the image.",35),
("OBNLM","optimized Bayesian non-local means","NLM variant for ultrasound (Coupé et al. 2009) whose patch distance is derived from a speckle noise model.",35),
("BM3D","block-matching and 3D filtering","Denoiser (Dabov et al. 2007) that groups similar patches into 3D stacks and shrinks them jointly in a transform domain.",35),
("BM4D","block-matching and 4D filtering","Volumetric extension of BM3D (Maggioni et al. 2013) that groups similar 3D cubes.",35),
("LMMSE","linear minimum mean squared error","Best linear estimator in the mean-squared sense; computed from local mean and variance it gives the Lee and Kuan speckle filters.",35),
("ENL","equivalent number of looks","Squared mean over variance of intensity in a homogeneous region: 1 for fully developed speckle, larger after averaging or despeckling.",35),
("ESF","edge spread function","Image of a sharp step edge; its 10–90 % width and its derivative (the LSF) measure resolution.",35),
("LSF","line spread function","Image of a thin line, equal to the derivative of the edge spread function; its FWHM is a resolution measure.",35),
("MIP","maximum intensity projection","Volume rendering that shows the largest voxel value along each ray; good for bright sparse targets such as contrast-filled vessels.",35),
("TF","transfer function (volume rendering)","The mapping from voxel value to colour and opacity in volume rendering.",35,[35]),
("ROC","receiver operating characteristic","Curve of true-positive rate against false-positive rate as a decision threshold varies; its area (AUC) summarizes detectability.",35),
("MRMC","multi-reader multi-case","Reader-study design in which several readers interpret the same cases, so conclusions generalize over both readers and cases.",35),
("SAR","synthetic-aperture radar","Radar imaging from a moving antenna; its speckle filters (Lee, Kuan, Frost) were adopted by ultrasound.",35,[35]),
("DL","deep learning","Machine learning with many-layer neural networks trained on data.",35,[35]),
("GAN","generative adversarial network","A generator trained against a discriminator; as a loss it can create realistic-looking but false image detail.",35),
("OOD","out-of-distribution","Inputs unlike the training data (other probes, sites or patients), on which learned filters can fail silently.",35),
("OCT","optical coherence tomography","Interferometric optical imaging with micrometre resolution; it shares fully developed speckle with ultrasound.",35),
# --- regulatory (FDA, EU) ---
("AI","artificial intelligence; additional information (FDA)","Two meanings: artificial intelligence (learned algorithms in device software, e.g. an AI view classifier), and FDA's additional-information request (AI letter) that puts a 510(k) on hold for up to 180 days.",36),
